.TH PATH 1 "OShell Built-in Commands"
.SH NAME
path \- set the shell's command search path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B path
[
.I directory ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B path
command sets OShell's internal search path for finding executable commands.
.PP
The search path is a list of directories where OShell looks for external commands.
Directories are searched in the order they are specified.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B path
clears the search path (no directories will be searched).
.PP
With directory arguments,
.B path
replaces the current search path with the specified directories.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B directory
One or more directory paths to add to the search path.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B path /bin /usr/bin
Set search path to /bin and /usr/bin.
.TP
.B path /bin /usr/bin /usr/local/bin
Set search path to three directories.
.TP
.B path
Clear the search path (external commands won't be found).
.SH DEFAULT PATH
When OShell starts, the default path is:
.RS
.IP
/bin
.br
/usr/bin
.br
/usr/local/bin
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The path command only affects OShell's internal path, not the $PATH environment variable
.IP \(bu 2
Absolute paths (starting with /) and relative paths (starting with ./) bypass the search path
.IP \(bu 2
If the search path is empty, only commands with absolute or relative paths can be executed
.IP \(bu 2
Maximum of 1024 directories can be in the path
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1)